  faqHeading: "Hiring an app developer in Dubai: the questions worth asking first",
  faqs: [
    { question: "What should I ask an app developer in Dubai before hiring them?", answer: "Four questions that filter most of the market. Which of the five jobs do you cover: mobile client, backend, release and signing, device testing, design? Whose developer account will publish it? Who generates and holds the signing keys? What bundle identifier will you use? A candidate who answers all four crisply has shipped apps that outlived the build." },
    { question: "Should my app be published under my own Apple and Google accounts?", answer: "Yes, always, and the accounts should exist before the first build. Enrol your own legal entity, because the enrolled organisation name is what appears publicly as the publisher, then invite the developer in with a revocable role. This costs you only the platform fees you would pay anyway and removes the whole category of problems around leaving a developer later." },
    { question: "Who should hold the Account Holder seat in App Store Connect?", answer: "Someone who will still work for your company in three years. The Account Holder can agree to new platform terms and ultimately controls the account, so it is not a role to hand to a supplier. Give your developer Admin or App Manager instead, which lets them create builds, manage metadata and submit, and which you can revoke in one click." },
    { question: "Can I change my app's bundle identifier or package name later?", answer: "No. Once an app is published, the identifier is permanent, and changing it means publishing a different app: a new store listing with zero installs, zero ratings and no update path for existing users. Agree the identifier in writing before the first build, using your own domain reversed rather than whatever your developer's template produces." },
    { question: "What happens if my developer loses the Android signing key?", answer: "It depends on the signing scheme. Under Play App Signing, Google holds the key users verify against and you sign uploads with a separate upload key, so a lost upload key is a replacement request. An older app signed with a single key held outside that scheme and then lost cannot be updated at all, and your only route is a new listing." },
    { question: "Can an app be transferred from my developer's account to mine later?", answer: "Usually, and both platforms have a process with conditions attached, but it needs the cooperation of the account currently holding the app, which is exactly what you have lost if the relationship broke down. It also means rebuilding push credentials, API keys and pipelines. Publishing under your own account from day one costs nothing and avoids the whole situation." },
    { question: "Native or cross-platform: which should I hire for?", answer: "Judge it as a replacement problem. Two native codebases means permanently needing both Swift and Kotlin capability. One cross-platform codebase halves that but concentrates risk in whoever wrote the native modules at the edges. Native specialists are the right hire when the device is the product: Bluetooth, camera-heavy work, augmented reality or continuous background location." },
    { question: "How should payments to an app developer be staged?", answer: "So that finishing is always worth more than leaving, with store review treated as its own stage. Small payment for a written specification, then interface direction approved on your own phone, then feature complete on your own test track, then the balance minus a holdback on first approval of a submission, then the holdback after a defined post-launch crash window." },
    { question: "Who pays if the app is rejected by the App Store?", answer: "It depends whose fault it was, and the split should be one written sentence before the first submission. A crash, a broken flow or a guideline the developer should know is their rework at their cost. A missing privacy policy you were going to write, a login wall with no reviewer test account, or a description promising a feature you cut is yours." },
    { question: "How much does app maintenance really cost after launch?", answer: `More in obligation than in money, and it is not optional. Android target level requirements rise annually, Apple periodically requires newer build tools, both platforms ship a major OS version each autumn, and certificates expire. With us, two months after launch are free, then upkeep starts from ${P.care} with the scope written into your quote. We do not offer 24/7 on-call.` },
    { question: "Do you have an office or a licence in Dubai?", answer: "No. BtechWaleTech is three freelance engineers working remotely from India, with no UAE office, no UAE trade licence, no UAE phone number and no staff in the country. We cannot attend a workshop, bid on a vendor portal that requires a licensed UAE supplier, or sit in a tender meeting. India is 1.5 hours ahead of Dubai, so calls happen inside your working day." },
    { question: "Should I build an app at all, or just a mobile website?", answer: "Build an app when it is opened repeatedly, needs the device for camera, offline storage, background location or meaningful push, or is for your own workforce. Build a mobile site when the audience transacts occasionally, because install friction is brutal and a few hundred occasional customers will not download anything. We will tell you which case you are in before quoting." },
  ],
